<%# The edge a panel is taken by, and the only part of it that is a handle.
    Everything else on a rail is a thing to press or a thing to read.

    The hand keeps hold of it wherever it then goes — over the content, over the
    next rail, past the window — because the pointer is captured on the way down.
    Without that, a hand that outran the edge would drop it somewhere in the
    middle of the room and the panel would stop following. %>
<%# It is also the honest answer to "can this panel be widened at all": the CSS
    takes it away on a phone, and a shut rail does not draw it. Where there is no
    grip, there is nothing to hold — and nothing holding the panel to the room. %>
<div class="grip <%= side %>" aria-hidden="true" data-panel-target="grip"
     data-action="pointerdown->panel#grab pointermove->panel#drag
                  pointerup->panel#drop pointercancel->panel#drop"></div>
